<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djoser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">27th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a> · Also known as Netjerikhet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djoser was a king of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Third Dynasty who reigned in the 27th century BCE. His Step Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, designed according to tradition by his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a>, was the first monumental stone building in Egypt. Its complex of courts, chapels and enclosure walls set a model for the royal tombs of the Old Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djoser.jpg" alt="Djoser" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomk2ski · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djoser_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Djoser, also read Zoser, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Third Dynasty and is often counted as the founder of the Old Kingdom. In contemporary inscriptions he is known by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name Netjerikhet, meaning divine of body. He was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasekhemwy">Khasekhemwy</a> and Queen Nimaathap, though whether he directly succeeded Khasekhemwy has been uncertain. The Egyptologist Toby Wilkinson argues from burial seals in Khasekhemwy's tomb at Abydos that Djoser was his direct successor and the founder of the dynasty.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives him a reign of twenty-nine years and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> nineteen. Wilkinson's reconstruction of the Royal Annals gives twenty-eight complete or partial years. Djoser sent expeditions to the Sinai Peninsula, where the local inhabitants were subdued and turquoise and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> were mined. The painted limestone statue of Djoser found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> in 1924 to 1925 is the oldest known life-sized Egyptian statue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His main monument is the step pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, the earliest colossal stone building in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It began as a nearly square mastaba, and five smaller steps were piled on top. It was once 62 metres high, with a base of about 125 by 109 metres, covered in polished white limestone. Beneath it lies a maze of corridors and chambers, with a burial chamber at the bottom of a shaft 28 metres deep, sealed by a plug stone of 3.5 tons. More than 40,000 stone vessels were found in the galleries.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a>, Djoser's head of the royal shipyard and overseer of all stone works, directed the construction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> credits Tosorthros with discovering how to build with hewn stone, which modern scholars think he meant to ascribe to Imhotep, later deified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Aesculapius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The date of his death is uncertain, and his relationship to his successor Sekhemkhet is not known. The Famine Stela, probably made in the Ptolemaic period but claiming to date from his reign, tells how Djoser rebuilt the temple of Khnum at Elephantine and so ended a seven-year famine. Some regard it as a legend. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> of the 2nd century CE from Tebtunis preserves a long demotic story about Djoser and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a>. The Egyptian historian al-Nuwayri, who lived from 1279 to 1333, described him as a wise ruler and said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> held a feast in his honour called the vine festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Djoser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first king of the Old Kingdom was <b>Djoser</b> of the Third Dynasty, who ruled some time between 2691 and 2625 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
